 Introduction
 ============

 What is Shogun?
 It's an interactive game, currently for up to 20 players, where the
 object for everyone is to be the greatest! <Surprise! :-D>

 Well, everything takes place in the 17:th century in Japan. The great
 empire are spread out in a lot of pieces, diffrent clans fight eachother
 and nobody knows who'll be the next Shogun. Of course, it must be a man
 that can rule the empire with a steady hand, but he must also take care
 of his people who puts all it's trust in the Shogun.

 There is also a federation between the greatest clans, and the members
 in that federation together makes sure everybody follows the old laws
 in their waiting for their new leaders. The federation may help you
 in your task but if you don't follow "the rules" they will turn
 against you.

 Ruling a japanese clan in search for the great throne - That's the game!

 Through the game, you'll pass a lot of phases which I'll describe a bit
 more carefully below. Hope you understand SOME of the information,
 although I'm rather *BAD* in english! <You'll notice!> :-/


 The "Phases"
 ============

 At first when logging on as a new user, you must fill in some information
 as what name you want etc. This part isn't much to tell about. It's
 easy...

 Every turn you'll at first be shown a brief summary of your assets, forces
 and that kind of stuff.

 Each day, you have 5 turns, you'll get these 5 new turns every night.

 Expenses
 --------
 Then, your headquarters (from where your clan is controlled), wants their
 part of this turn's profits.
 Your military forces also wanna get payed, and you'd better pay them,
 else they may not want to fight for you!

 Food
 ----
 Your rice-field produces food. If you've got many of them you'll at this
 point be able to sell some kilotons of food and make yourself some money.
 And of course, if you've got a lack of 'em you'll be able to buy some!
 Make sure you have enough food. Your people don't like to starve!

 After the "food-market" you are about to decide how much food they will
 get this turn. You will be told how much they want, but as this is an
 absolute minimum amount you really should give them more to make sure
 they like you.

 (The more the people like you -> The more they become! People can even
  move from other clans if you're kind enough! :-D)

 The Market
 ==========
 Here we can buy a lot of things for the last money we've saved...

 Troops
 ------
 Troops are both offensive and defencive. They are cheap but aren't as
 strong as you might want. :-/  They are, anyhow, needed to build up a
 complete military force...

 Armour
 ------
 Armour-sets aren't only for the soldiers, it may also be other things
 that protects your whole forces against an attacker.
 The armour is only for defence, but you can, and its good, to have
 more of them than you have troops.

 Samurais
 --------
 These are the strongest of your warriors. They will both defend you and
 help you in attacks. They are more expensive than the troops but a lot
 stronger too...

 Riders
 ------
 The riders are good. Not quite as good as the samurais and therefor
 also a bit cheaper. Each rider needs one horse to be able to fight.
 They'll both protect you and help you in your raids...
 If you don't have horses for your riders (see below) they are useless.

 Horses
 ------
 Necessary for the riders described above.

 Rice-Fields
 -----------
 The fields produces food. Not too good having too many of them. Better
 putting your money in the villages because you don't earn that much
 money in selling food.

 Craftsmen Villages
 ------------------
 These are the working ones. They are your biggest income-source and
 they are the ones you really should put your money in the first turns
 that you anyway are protected! If you do, you'll get a lot of money
 and can then begin to spend them on your army etc... Just a little
 hint.

 Headquarters
 ------------
 You need (_MUST HAVE_!) one headquarter/10 craftsmen villages.
 If you don't the people may be confused and feel they are the police
 in your clan.. <Grin>  But don't have TOO many, they cost you a
 fortune every turn.

 The Bank
 ========
 From the market you can visit the bank.
 Not much to say about the bank. Make sure to pay back 10% of your
 loan every turn, else the federation may take something from your
 assets! :-(
 You'll get 5% rent for money you put in the bank. You can save your
 money here if you suspect somebody may want to rob your cash-assets.
 (They may rob the bank too, even if it's harder! :-D)

 Special Missions
 ================
 The cost a lot but they will make a rather great damage on your enemy.
 There are a few of them and you'll find out what each stands for.
 One mission/turn is allowed.
 You won't win anything DIRECTLY on a saboutage but you maybe just
 wanna be a pain in the ass for your enemies. :-)

 Battle
 ======
 You may want to attack someone. The smartest thing to do, often, is to
 attack the ones that are as big as you, or little smaller. Then you'll
 probably win and maybe gain some villages or whatever you're after.

 (You'll be able to chose what your raid's quest will be!)

 Pressing RETURN through the requests about how many of your soldiers
 you want to send, will result in that you send ALL of them.

 NOTE: It's NOT good to send all if you expect the fight to be easy for
 you. You can limit your losses by just sending enough soldiers to win.

 Sometimes the federation offers you to hire samurais. Could be a good
 thing to do if they are cheap. Sometimes they aren't...

 When you win, your soldiers will be glad. :-)
 When you loose, they won't be glad. :-(

 A happy soldier fights better than a sad one. That's something to think
 about. Always be kind to them... :-)

 If you win you'll see what you found. If you for example were going for
 their bank you'll see how much you find. <Yeah!>

 Turn Summary
 ============
 This summary shows how things were going this past turn.
 (No more to be said really...)

 Messages
 ========
 Yep. You may send messages to your opponents!
 Hate 'em, admire 'em... Make them attack the same opponent as you
 may have!   / Or maybe send 'em loveletters? <Each!> :-)


 Then everything will start over again!

 <That's It?>

 Yep, it maybe sounds rather complicated, but the more you get into
 the game the more options you want!

